/target
/build

# Cargo.lock, so every build resolves the day dependency fresh and gets the tip of the branch it
# tracks (`branch = "main"` in Cargo.toml). A committed lock would pin one day revision until
# someone ran `cargo update`. CI resolves its own: the shared workflow's `Prepare Cargo.lock`
# step writes one when none is committed, and the pristine check ignores it.
/Cargo.lock

# Machine-local: `day patch --local <day-checkout>` writes this to build against a checkout
# instead of the git dependency. CI has no such file and resolves git.
/.cargo/config.toml

# Xcode local state. The .xcodeproj itself is source; what Xcode writes inside it is not:
# project.xcworkspace holds the SwiftPM resolution for the pieces package day generates under
# build/, and cannot be relocated out of the project.
xcuserdata/
DerivedData/
**/*.xcodeproj/project.xcworkspace/

# Per-checkout Xcode settings: each platform/<p>/DayApp.xcconfig ends with an optional
# `#include? "DayApp.local.xcconfig"`, so a developer can set their signing team (and anything
# else this machine needs) without committing it. Absent on a fresh clone and in CI, which is
# why the include is optional.
platform/*/DayApp.local.xcconfig

# Gradle local state, the android counterpart of the above: platform/android/ is source, but
# what Gradle generates inside it is not. Gradle writes its caches and per-module build/ trees
# into the project it builds, so they cannot move under build/ with the rest of day's outputs;
# `/build` at the top of this file is anchored to the repo root and never matches them. Left
# untracked they fail the pristine check CI runs before packing an artifact.
platform/android/.gradle/
platform/android/**/build/
platform/android/local.properties
# Gradle's `updateDaemonJvm` task writes this when an IDE syncs the project: a daemon JVM vendor
# and version chosen on one machine, with download URLs. A project that means to pin its daemon
# JVM commits it by hand (`git add -f`).
platform/android/gradle/gradle-daemon-jvm.properties
# Android Studio writes its project model into the Gradle root it opens. The Xcode counterpart
# (xcuserdata/) is above; this is the same thing for the other IDE, and without it the first
# person to open platform/android in Studio leaves a dozen untracked files behind.
platform/android/.idea/

# The website is generated from repo data: the daysite template clone (local preview) and the
# synthesized data files. Only website/site.toml and website/theme.css are source.
website/.daysite/
website/appindex.json
website/channels.json
website/gallery-manifest.json
# The same pair again under each extra build channel's own directory (website/main/).
website/*/appindex.json
website/*/gallery-manifest.json

# HarmonyOS reads its icon media from a fixed place under each module, so `day prepare` links
# these two directories to the generated set under build/day/host/harmony/media.
/platform/harmony/entry/src/main/resources/base/media
/platform/harmony/AppScope/resources/base/media

**/__pycache__/

